# React Flow Framework Best Practices

<version>2.0.0</version>

## Context
- When building node-based UIs, diagrams, flowcharts, or graph-based applications with React Flow
- When implementing canvas, nodes, edges, layouts, interactions, or styling for React Flow applications
- When setting up a new React Flow project or extending an existing one

## Requirements

### Installation and Setup
- Use the latest stable React Flow version (currently v12.4.x)
- Install React Flow with `npm install @xyflow/react` or equivalent for your package manager
- Import components as named imports: `import { ReactFlow, Controls, Background } from '@xyflow/react'`
- Always import the required CSS styles: `import '@xyflow/react/dist/style.css'`
- For custom styling without React Flow defaults, import only base styles: `import '@xyflow/react/dist/base.css'`
- Wrap your application with `ReactFlowProvider` when using React Flow hooks outside the `ReactFlow` component
- Initialize React Flow with proper viewport settings and dimensions (typically 100% width/height of container)

### General rule
- When implementing a feature with React Flow, always do some upfront research on how the feature might be implemented with the latest, standard React Flow, be creative, but adhere to the rules. In case you have to resort to implementing custom solutions, try to incorporate and build on React Flow.

### Canvas Implementation
- Use the `ReactFlow` component as the main container for your flow
- Include essential props: `nodes`, `edges`, `onNodesChange`, `onEdgesChange`, and `onConnect`
- Add viewport controls with the `Controls` component for better user experience
- Include a `Background` component for visual clarity
- Use `fitView` prop to automatically fit the viewport to the graph content
- Set appropriate `defaultViewport` or use `fitView` with custom options when needed
- Consider using the `colorMode` prop ("light", "dark", or "system") for built-in theme support

<example>
```tsx
import { ReactFlow, Controls, Background, Panel } from '@xyflow/react';
import '@xyflow/react/dist/style.css';

function Flow() {
  // State management code here...
  
  return (
    <div style={{ width: '100%', height: '500px' }}>
      <ReactFlow
        nodes={nodes}
        edges={edges}
        onNodesChange={onNodesChange}
        onEdgesChange={onEdgesChange}
        onConnect={onConnect}
        fitView
        colorMode="light" // or "dark" or "system"
      >
        <Controls />
        <Background variant="dots" gap={12} size={1} />
        <Panel position="top-left">My Flow</Panel>
      </ReactFlow>
    </div>
  );
}
```
</example>

### Node Implementation
- Define nodes with unique `id`, `position`, and `data` properties
- Use the `type` property to specify custom node types
- Register custom node types with the `nodeTypes` prop on `ReactFlow`
- Implement custom nodes as React components that receive `data` and other node props
- Use `Handle` components with proper `type` (source/target) and `position` for connections
- Implement node resizing with `NodeResizer` when appropriate
- Use `NodeToolbar` for contextual actions on nodes
- For server-side rendering, explicitly define `width` and `height` properties
- Understand the difference between explicitly set dimensions (`width`/`height`) and measured dimensions (`measured.width`/`measured.height`)
- Use `parentId` (not `parentNode`) to specify parent-child relationships for nested nodes

<example>
```tsx
// Node definition
const nodes = [
  {
    id: 'node-1',
    type: 'custom',
    position: { x: 100, y: 100 },
    // Optional explicit dimensions (useful for SSR)
    width: 150,
    height: 50,
    data: { label: 'Custom Node' },
    // For nested nodes
    parentId: 'parent-node-id', // Note: parentNode is now parentId in v12
  }
];

// Custom node component
function CustomNode({ data, id }) {
  // Access measured dimensions if needed
  // const { width, height } = useInternalNode(id).measured;
  
  return (
    <div className="custom-node">
      <div>{data.label}</div>
      <Handle type="target" position={Position.Top} />
      <Handle type="source" position={Position.Bottom} />
    </div>
  );
}

// Register node type
const nodeTypes = {
  custom: CustomNode,
};
```
</example>

### Edge Implementation
- Define edges with unique `id`, `source`, and `target` properties
- Use the `type` property to specify custom edge types
- Register custom edge types with the `edgeTypes` prop on `ReactFlow`
- Implement custom edges as React components that receive edge props
- Use appropriate marker types for edge ends (e.g., `MarkerType.Arrow`)
- Use `getBezierPath` or other path utilities for custom edge paths
- Implement edge labels with `EdgeLabelRenderer` for better performance
- Use `reconnectEdge` utility (renamed from `updateEdge` in v12) for edge reconnection

<example>
```tsx
// Edge definition
const edges = [
  {
    id: 'edge-1',
    type: 'custom',
    source: 'node-1',
    target: 'node-2',
    markerEnd: { type: MarkerType.ArrowClosed },
  }
];

// Custom edge component
function CustomEdge({ id, source, target, markerEnd, style }) {
  const [edgePath] = getBezierPath({
    sourceX, sourceY, targetX, targetY,
  });
  
  return (
    <>
      <path id={id} className="react-flow__edge-path" d={edgePath} style={style} />
      <EdgeLabelRenderer>
        <div
          style={{
            position: 'absolute',
            transform: `translate(-50%, -50%) translate(${labelX}px,${labelY}px)`,
            pointerEvents: 'all',
          }}
          className="nodrag nopan"
        >
          Edge Label
        </div>
      </EdgeLabelRenderer>
    </>
  );
}

// Register edge type
const edgeTypes = {
  custom: CustomEdge,
};
```
</example>

### Layout Implementation
- Use external libraries for automatic layouts (dagre, elkjs, d3-force)
- Implement layout functions that modify node positions without changing other properties
- Apply layouts on initial render or when graph structure changes
- Use `ReactFlowProvider` and `useReactFlow` hook to access the `fitView` function after layout
- Consider performance implications for large graphs and use memoization
- Implement sub-flows or nested graphs for complex hierarchical structures
- Use the new `getNodesBounds` utility from `useReactFlow` hook for determining node bounds

<example>
```tsx
// Using dagre for automatic layout
import dagre from 'dagre';
import { useCallback } from 'react';
import { useNodesState, useEdgesState, useReactFlow } from '@xyflow/react';

function LayoutFlow() {
  const [nodes, setNodes, onNodesChange] = useNodesState(initialNodes);
  const [edges, setEdges, onEdgesChange] = useEdgesState(initialEdges);
  const { fitView, getNodesBounds } = useReactFlow();
  
  const getLayoutedElements = useCallback((nodes, edges, direction = 'TB') => {
    const dagreGraph = new dagre.graphlib.Graph();
    dagreGraph.setDefaultEdgeLabel(() => ({}));
    dagreGraph.setGraph({ rankdir: direction });
    
    // Add nodes to dagre
    nodes.forEach((node) => {
      // Use measured dimensions if available, otherwise use explicit or default dimensions
      const width = node.width || node.measured?.width || 150;
      const height = node.height || node.measured?.height || 50;
      dagreGraph.setNode(node.id, { width, height });
    });
    
    // Add edges to dagre
    edges.forEach((edge) => {
      dagreGraph.setEdge(edge.source, edge.target);
    });
    
    // Calculate layout
    dagre.layout(dagreGraph);
    
    // Apply layout to nodes
    return {
      nodes: nodes.map((node) => {
        const nodeWithPosition = dagreGraph.node(node.id);
        return {
          ...node,
          position: {
            x: nodeWithPosition.x - (node.width || node.measured?.width || 150) / 2,
            y: nodeWithPosition.y - (node.height || node.measured?.height || 50) / 2,
          },
        };
      }),
      edges,
    };
  }, []);
  
  const onLayout = useCallback((direction) => {
    const { nodes: layoutedNodes, edges: layoutedEdges } = getLayoutedElements(
      nodes,
      edges,
      direction
    );
    
    setNodes([...layoutedNodes]);
    setEdges([...layoutedEdges]);
    
    window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
      fitView({ padding: 0.2 });
    });
  }, [nodes, edges, setNodes, setEdges, fitView, getLayoutedElements]);
  
  return (
    <ReactFlow
      nodes={nodes}
      edges={edges}
      onNodesChange={onNodesChange}
      onEdgesChange={onEdgesChange}
      fitView
    >
      <Panel position="top-right">
        <button onClick={() => onLayout('TB')}>Vertical Layout</button>
        <button onClick={() => onLayout('LR')}>Horizontal Layout</button>
      </Panel>
    </ReactFlow>
  );
}
```
</example>

### Interaction Implementation
- Use `onConnect` handler to process new connections
- Implement `onNodesChange` and `onEdgesChange` handlers for node/edge modifications
- Use `ConnectionMode.Loose` for more flexible connections or `ConnectionMode.Strict` for validation
- Set appropriate interaction flags: `nodesDraggable`, `nodesConnectable`, `elementsSelectable`
- Implement custom connection validation with `isValidConnection` prop
- Use `onConnectStart` and `onConnectEnd` for custom connection behavior
- Use `onReconnect` (renamed from `onEdgeUpdate` in v12) for edge reconnection
- Implement undo/redo functionality for user actions

<example>
```tsx
const Flow = () => {
  const [nodes, setNodes, onNodesChange] = useNodesState(initialNodes);
  const [edges, setEdges, onEdgesChange] = useEdgesState(initialEdges);
  
  const onConnect = useCallback(
    (params) => setEdges((eds) => addEdge(params, eds)),
    [setEdges]
  );
  
  const onReconnect = useCallback(
    (oldEdge, newConnection) => {
      setEdges((els) => reconnectEdge(oldEdge, newConnection, els));
    },
    [setEdges]
  );
  
  const isValidConnection = useCallback(
    (connection) => {
      // Custom validation logic
      return connection.source !== connection.target;
    },
    []
  );
  
  return (
    <ReactFlow
      nodes={nodes}
      edges={edges}
      onNodesChange={onNodesChange}
      onEdgesChange={onEdgesChange}
      onConnect={onConnect}
      onReconnect={onReconnect}
      isValidConnection={isValidConnection}
      connectionMode={ConnectionMode.Loose}
      nodesDraggable={true}
      nodesConnectable={true}
      elementsSelectable={true}
    />
  );
};
```
</example>

### State Management Best Practices
- Use the built-in `useNodesState` and `useEdgesState` hooks for efficient state management
- Leverage the `addEdge` utility function for consistent edge creation
- Ensure all callbacks are wrapped with `useCallback` to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- Use the `applyNodeChanges` and `applyEdgeChanges` utilities when manually handling changes
- Keep node and edge state outside of the ReactFlow component for better control
- Use the `ReactFlowProvider` at the top level when accessing React Flow hooks outside the main component
- Avoid direct DOM manipulation for node creation; always update the state instead
- Use the `defaultEdgeOptions` prop for consistent edge styling instead of adding styles to each edge
- For reactive flows, use `useNodeConnections` (successor to `useHandleConnections`) and `useNodesData` hooks

<example>
```tsx
// Recommended state management pattern
import { 
  ReactFlow, 
  useNodesState, 
  useEdgesState, 
  addEdge,
  ReactFlowProvider,
  useNodeConnections,
  useNodesData
} from '@xyflow/react';
import '@xyflow/react/dist/style.css';

const initialNodes = [/* ... */];
const initialEdges = [/* ... */];

function Flow() {
  const [nodes, setNodes, onNodesChange] = useNodesState(initialNodes);
  const [edges, setEdges, onEdgesChange] = useEdgesState(initialEdges);
  
  const onConnect = useCallback(
    (params) => setEdges((eds) => addEdge(params, eds)),
    [setEdges]
  );
  
  const addNewNode = useCallback(
    (type, position) => {
      const newNode = {
        id: `node-${Date.now()}`,
        type,
        position,
        data: { /* ... */ },
      };
      setNodes((nds) => [...nds, newNode]);
    },
    [setNodes]
  );
  
  return (
    <ReactFlow
      nodes={nodes}
      edges={edges}
      onNodesChange={onNodesChange}
      onEdgesChange={onEdgesChange}
      onConnect={onConnect}
      defaultEdgeOptions={{
        type: 'smoothstep',
        animated: true,
      }}
    >
      {/* ... */}
    </ReactFlow>
  );
}

// Example of a reactive node using useNodeConnections
function ReactiveNode({ id, data }) {
  // Get all connections to this node
  const connections = useNodeConnections(id);
  
  // Or filter by handle type
  const targetConnections = useNodeConnections(id, { handleType: 'target' });
  
  // Access data from connected nodes
  const connectedNodesData = useNodesData(
    connections.map((connection) => connection.nodeId)
  );
  
  // Process data from connected nodes
  const processedData = useMemo(() => {
    // Process data from connected nodes
    return connectedNodesData.reduce((acc, nodeData) => {
      // Your data processing logic
      return acc + (nodeData?.value || 0);
    }, 0);
  }, [connectedNodesData]);
  
  return (
    <div>
      <div>Value: {processedData}</div>
      <Handle type="target" position={Position.Top} />
      <Handle type="source" position={Position.Bottom} />
    </div>
  );
}

// Wrap with provider when needed
export default function App() {
  return (
    <ReactFlowProvider>
      <Flow />
    </ReactFlowProvider>
  );
}
```
</example>

### Server-Side Rendering
- For SSR/SSG support, explicitly define `width` and `height` for nodes
- Define handle positions explicitly for accurate connections in SSR
- Use the `nodeOrigin` prop to control the origin point of nodes
- Understand that measured dimensions will be available after hydration
- Consider using the `nodesDraggable={false}` prop for static SSR flows

<example>
```tsx
// SSR-compatible node definition
const nodes = [
  {
    id: 'node-1',
    type: 'custom',
    position: { x: 100, y: 100 },
    // Explicit dimensions for SSR
    width: 150,
    height: 50,
    data: { label: 'SSR Node' },
  }
];

// SSR-compatible React Flow component
function SSRFlow() {
  return (
    <ReactFlow
      nodes={nodes}
      edges={edges}
      onNodesChange={onNodesChange}
      onEdgesChange={onEdgesChange}
      onConnect={onConnect}
      // Optional: set node origin for positioning
      nodeOrigin={[0.5, 0.5]} // Center of the node
    />
  );
}
```
</example>

### Computing Flows (Reactive Data)
- Use `useNodeConnections` hook to track connections to a node (replaces `useHandleConnections`)
- Use `useNodesData` hook to access data from connected nodes
- Implement reactive data flows between connected nodes
- Consider performance implications for large graphs and use memoization
- Use the `updateNode` and `updateNodeData` functions for updating node data

<example>
```tsx
// Reactive node implementation
function ComputingNode({ id, data }) {
  const { getNode, setNodes } = useReactFlow();
  
  // Get all incoming connections
  const incomingConnections = useNodeConnections(id, { handleType: 'target' });
  
  // Get data from connected source nodes
  const sourceNodesData = useNodesData(
    incomingConnections.map((connection) => connection.nodeId)
  );
  
  // Compute result based on incoming data
  const result = useMemo(() => {
    return sourceNodesData.reduce((sum, nodeData) => {
      return sum + (nodeData?.value || 0);
    }, 0);
  }, [sourceNodesData]);
  
  // Update this node's data when result changes
  useEffect(() => {
    setNodes((nds) =>
      nds.map((node) => {
        if (node.id === id) {
          return {
            ...node,
            data: {
              ...node.data,
              computedValue: result,
            },
          };
        }
        return node;
      })
    );
  }, [id, result, setNodes]);
  
  return (
    <div>
      <div>Input Sum: {result}</div>
      <div>Computed: {data.computedValue}</div>
      <Handle type="target" position={Position.Top} />
      <Handle type="source" position={Position.Bottom} />
    </div>
  );
}
```
</example>

### Theming and Dark Mode
- Use the `colorMode` prop ("light", "dark", or "system") for built-in theme support
- Override default styles with CSS variables for consistent theming
- Use Tailwind CSS or Styled Components for custom styling
- Implement responsive designs for different screen sizes
- Use the new CSS variables for customizing colors and styles

